Explicit casts target compiler-supported builtin types:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Bool
    Int
    String
    Char
    Float
    Error
]

Numeric targets are the profile-selected `Int` and `Float`, the fixed integer
and binary float types, `Byte` and the `Number` family.

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    integers:      Int, I8, I16, I32, I64, U8, U16, U32, U64
    binary floats: Float, F16, F32, F64
    other:         Byte, Number, Number0, Number1 through Number256
]

A type being a valid target does not mean every source type can convert to it.

### Builtin cast table

- `Int -> Float` (infallible): Numeric conversion to finite `Float`
- `Int -> String` (infallible): Base-10 signed integer text
- `Float -> String` (infallible): Moth's stable float formatter
- `Bool -> String` (infallible): `"true"` or `"false"`
- `Char -> String` (infallible): One Unicode scalar as UTF-8 text
- `Char -> Int` (infallible): Unicode scalar code point
- `String -> Error` (infallible): Error message from the text with code `0`
- `Error -> String` (infallible): The error message only
- `Float -> Int` (fallible): Truncate toward zero, then require the `Int` range
- `Int -> Char` (fallible): Require a valid Unicode scalar value
- `String -> Int` (fallible): Parse the complete Moth integer text grammar
- `String -> Float` (fallible): Parse the complete numeric grammar and require a finite result
- `String -> Bool` (fallible): Trim surrounding whitespace, then accept exactly lowercase `true` or `false`
- `String -> Char` (fallible): Require exactly one Unicode scalar

Numeric conversion is then classified by the complete source and target types.

- Fixed integer to fixed integer is infallible when the complete source range fits the target range and fallible otherwise. `U8 -> U16` and `U8 -> I16` are infallible. `U16 -> U8` and `I8 -> U8` are fallible.
- `Int`, `Float` and fixed-width values require an explicit cast between distinct types, even at a matching representation. Infallibility follows the complete ranges, so `Int -> I32` is infallible only when the selected `Int` width fits `I32`.
- Integer to `F32` or `F64` is infallible and may round. Integer to `F16` is infallible only when the complete source range fits the `F16` finite range, so `I32 -> F16` is a fallible finite-range conversion.
- A conversion to a binary float of greater precision is infallible. A conversion to a narrower precision is fallible and rounds once, directly from the source precision. `F64 -> F32`, `F32 -> F16` and `F64 -> F16` are all fallible, and `F64 -> F16` is never a two-step `F64 -> F32 -> F16` conversion. `Float` uses the precision selected by the numeric profile.
- Float to integer is fallible. It truncates toward zero, then checks the target range. `F64 -> I64` is one example.
- `Byte -> U8` and `U8 -> Byte` are infallible in both directions. Other numeric `Byte` conversions compose through `U8` rather than forming a separate conversion lattice.
- Integer to `Number` or any `NumberN` is exact and infallible. Scale widening is exact and infallible.
- Scale narrowing and `Number` or `NumberN` to integer are fallible exact conversions. Removed fractional digits must be zero and the integer must fit its explicit or profile-selected target.
- Every numeric target accepts `String` through the complete numeric text grammar (fallible). `Number` and `NumberN` parsing is exact at the target scale.
- Every numeric target converts to `String` (infallible) through its canonical text. `Byte` text conversion composes through `U8`, covering parsing and formatting.

No other builtin source-target pair is implied by the target list.

Same-type casts are invalid.

### Numeric conversion exactness

A cast does not promise universal exactness. Binary float conversions round to
nearest, ties-to-even. Float to integer truncates toward zero before the integer
range check, so this conversion does not use the float-narrowing failure rule.
Precision loss alone never fails a binary-float precision narrowing, and only a
rounded non-finite result fails one.

Integer-to-integer conversion preserves the value exactly. `Number` scale
widening is exact; scale narrowing and `Number`-to-integer conversion are exact
or fail, never rounded. Same-type casts remain invalid.

No modulo, wrapping or saturating conversion is added to the language.

### Implementation status

This reference owns the accepted cast contract. @../progress (Progress) owns
current compiler and backend support. The Alpha compiler resolves the existing
`Bool`, `Int`, `String`, `Char`, `Float` and `Error` targets only.

### Target source

The target must come from the immediate typed boundary.

Valid target boundaries include:

- an annotated declaration or assignment
- an explicit return slot
- a concrete function parameter
- a struct or choice field
- a default value
- a typed collection or map entry
- a compiler-owned typed assertion argument slot (`condition: Bool` or `message: String?`)
- a `then` arm whose enclosing value-producing block has an explicit receiver

Generic parameter slots are not cast targets. Generic inference does not look
through `cast`.

These positions do not supply cast targets:

- conditions
- loop conditions
- template interpolation
- operator operands
- expression statements
- untyped declarations

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- INVALID: these positions do not supply cast targets
    value = cast source

    if cast value:
        io.line("invalid")
    ;

    [: [cast value]]
]

The forms above are invalid because no immediate builtin target exists.

### Optional receiving contexts

A `T?` receiving context casts to inner `T`, then applies normal contextual
wrapping to `T?`.

Optional source values are not automatically unwrapped.

In:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    target T? = cast source catch:
        then fallback
    ;
]

the recovery handler produces inner `T`. `then none` is invalid because
recovery happens before optional wrapping.

### Numeric and text details

- `String -> Int`, `String -> Float` and every other numeric text conversion consume the whole string.
- Numeric text rejects surrounding whitespace, uppercase exponent markers, unary plus, malformed separators, malformed exponents, `NaN` and infinity spellings.
- Binary float text conversion additionally rejects a grammar-valid non-finite result.
- Every integer target materialises inside its own range. The `Int` range follows the selected profile.
- Binary float to `String` normalises negative zero to `0` and uses lowercase exponent formatting when exponential notation is required.
- `Number` to `String` uses canonical `Number` text with fractional trailing zeroes trimmed, no exponent notation, no negative zero and a leading zero before a fractional value.
- `Byte` and `U8` remain nominally distinct. No receiving boundary widens one into the other silently.
- Contextual `Int -> Float` promotion remains separate from explicit casting, though explicit `cast` is valid from a naturally `Int` source to a `Float` target.
